functor: 1
title: Editor and preview
---

set heading(numbering: none)

The website and the desktop app have the same editor. The source is at the left. The preview is at the right.

# Go between the source and the preview

| You do | Result |
|:--|:--|
| Double-click a word in the preview | The cursor goes to that word in the source, and the word is selected. |
| Double-click math in the preview | The source of that math is selected. |
| Press `Alt+Enter`, or click **Locate** | The preview scrolls to the cursor. The word or the math at the cursor flashes. |
| Move the cursor | A light band in the preview marks the block that holds the cursor. |

```text
 source line 74  ──► block with data-fn-line="73" data-fn-end-line="75"
        ▲                         │
        │                         ▼
 k-th "related" in lines 73-75 ◄── k-th "related" in the block text
```

# See the current section

When you scroll the preview, a bar at its top shows the section that you read. It shows the number and the title of each level, for example:

```text
 2 Vectors  ›  2.3 Inner products
```

Click a part of the bar to go to the start of that section. The bar is hidden above the first heading. HTML pages that `functor compile` writes have the same bar. Print and the PDF do not show it.

# Spelling

The editor checks the spelling of prose. A wavy line marks a word that is not in the dictionary. Math, code, labels, references, declarations and URLs are not checked.

| You do | Result |
|:--|:--|
| Right-click a marked word, or press `Ctrl+.` (`Cmd+.` on a Mac) | A menu shows suggestions and **Add to my word list**. |
| Press `Alt+F7` | The next marked word is selected. |
| Click **Spelling** | Turn the check on or off, choose English (UK) or English (US), and remove words from your word list. |

Your word list stays in this browser or in the desktop app. It is not in the file.

# How it works

Each block in the HTML output tells which source lines it comes from.

| Attribute | Where | Value |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `data-fn-line`, `data-fn-end-line` | a top-level block | the first and the last source line |
| `data-fn-rel` | a block inside `:::` | the lines, relative to the top-level block, for example `1-3` |

The editor finds the block, and then finds the same word in the lines of that block. Math is found by its source text. So a jump is exact to the word, but a word that the line breaker splits with a hyphen goes to the start of its block.

The PDF has no jumps. Use the preview to find a place, then export the PDF.
